00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.720
 Our press secretary gave alternative facts to that.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:51.920
 My goal in this deposition was to be truthful, but not particularly helpful.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.080
 Welcome to Unspun, the podcast that makes you better at finding the truth.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.360
 The way people get news is changing.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.200
 It used to be that there were many reporters who would research stories and write articles,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:08.280
 but now politicians and famous people share information directly with you on social media and the internet.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.680
 That means you find out things fast, but it's up to you to make sure the information is actually accurate.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.880
 And newsmakers don't always do their part.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.080
 The temptation to manipulate information is strong.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.520
 They bend the truth to deceive so that they can avoid accountability, so that they can advance their agendas.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.200
 When you recognize these agendas, you can sometimes find out what's real.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.760
 And we're at a crossroads where anyone can share anything online,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.200
 so it's important to sharpen your critical thinking skills.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.880
 Finding that deception before it goes viral is pretty much a survival skill now,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.320
 and we're going to do it together.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.800
 Let's get Unspun.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.040
 Hello, everyone, and welcome to this week's Unspun.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.800
 In this episode, you'll learn to recognize some ways that newsmakers use distraction to shape the news you see.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.240
 Then when interviews and press conferences are not enough,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.480
 journalists can use databases to find questions and answers that help hold the powerful to account.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.800
 So a lot of data journalist Ben Welsh will join me for the interview.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.560
 Sound good? Let's get Unspun.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.800
 Imagine this. Your child has maybe been taking it easy and they're not doing so well in school.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.760
 Education is important to you, and you know he's pretty capable,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.480
 so you expect pretty good marks on the next report card.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.520
 But your child knows that the next one you see is going to be really disappointing.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.120
 For several days before that report arrives home,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.080
 he pays extra attention to his contributions in keeping the home clean.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.680
 He cleans his room maybe, he remembers to take his shoes off at the door.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.920
 Somehow his dishes manage to make it from the table into the dishwasher without you even having to remind him.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.280
 The day comes and you open up that mail from the school.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.320
 You look up from that disappointing lineup and you start to say something,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.200
 and he interrupts to say, "Hey, have you seen my room? It's very clean."

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.200
 Your child is hoping you will fall for distraction.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.240
 Distraction tactics are powerful.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.640
 They can change what people think and they hide important problems.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:05.560
 By understanding these tactics, we can see the difference between real talk and lies.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.560
 Distraction works by taking advantage of how our brains work.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.760
 It's hard to focus on many things at once.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.680
 When something new or exciting pops up, we pay attention to that instead.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.280
 And this lets people hide things they don't want us to pay attention to.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.760
 The news and social media make this worse.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.080
 News needs to be new and exciting to get our attention,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.760
 and that means big flashy stories, even if they're not important, they get shown more.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.160
 And social media doesn't help, because it learns what we like and it shows us more of that.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.880
 Which means we might only see things we already agree with, even if they're not true.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.680
 This is bad for everyone.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.280
 When we don't talk about real problems, they don't get fixed.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.080
 Distraction stops us from making good choices because we don't have all the facts.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.320
 We need to be careful and look for what's hidden when things feel like they're too simple,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.880
 or even just too exciting.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.240
 So let's unpack this in the political world.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.200
 We'll listen to a few examples for this week's warm-up.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.480
 Have a listen and see if you can identify the distraction.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.480
 Around the globe, the president realized that our current approach to testing

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.560
 was inadequate to meet the needs of the American public.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.880
 He asked for an entire overhaul of the testing approach.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.200
 He immediately called the private sector laboratories to the White House, as noted,

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.840
 and charged them with developing a high-throughput quality platform

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.720
 that can meet the needs of the American public.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.600
 We are grateful to LabCorp and Quest for taking up the charge immediately after the meeting,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.640
 and within 72 hours bringing additional testing access,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:42.160
 particularly to the outbreak areas of Washington State and California and now across the country.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.880
 Ah, 2020, the early days of COVID.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.520
 Maybe you remember the uncertainty, the fear,

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.440
 if you live in the United States, the daily press conferences.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.600
 This clip beautifully illustrates a classic logical fallacy, the fallacy of distraction.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.560
 So here's how it works.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.920
 Instead of addressing the actual issue at hand,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.240
 in this case, a lack of COVID testing, a shiny, irrelevant fact shows up.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.920
 Hey, companies are helping us.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.960
 All the things that are being described here are plans that have just started,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.120
 but the situation was not good at the time.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:21.120
 In fact, this is the same speech in which the president declared a national health emergency.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.000
 Now, imagine this playing out in the whirlwind of the 24/7 news cycle.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.120
 Headlines are changing at lightning speed and there's a constant influx of information,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.320
 and it makes it difficult to focus on any one thing for too long.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.000
 This environment is fertile ground for the distraction fallacy to thrive.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.400
 Before we even have time to process the lack of testing,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:44.120
 we're already swept away by the promise of a great new website from Google.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.760
 And then enter social media algorithms designed to feed us more of what we already engage with,

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.800
 you know, creating those echo chambers and those filter bubbles.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.560
 So when a politician or an organization successfully deploys a distraction

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.840
 that aligns with our existing biases, the algorithm amplifies it.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.520
 But, you know, this strategy, this well-timed distraction is nothing new.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.440
 So here's another example.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.000
 Picture this, it's 1998, the Bill Clinton era,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.080
 and the House is getting ready to vote on impeaching the president.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.920
 A member of the House of Representatives gets up and says this.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.040
 Where do we go from here?

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:27.760
 We will go to the floor of the House of Representatives to debate this issue.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.200
 It is going to be a nasty debate.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.680
 It will be a debate about sex.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.040
 It will be a debate about the private affair of the president.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.480
 It will be a debate about the extramarital affair.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.640
 It will be a debate that the American public may not necessarily want to hear.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:52.160
 But they have been thrust into this debate by members of this House

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.000
 and the Republican Party who are intent and who will stop at nothing

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.320
 to bring down the president of the United States of America.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.160
 So, you know, at this time, the Monica Lewinsky scandal is exploding.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.800
 And impeachment hearings are on everybody's mind.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.560
 The actual grounds for the impeachment were perjury.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.160
 Clinton, when he was being deposed for a sexual harassment lawsuit,

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.880
 denied having sex with Monica Lewinsky.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.120
 And later, evidence emerged proving that he lied

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:24.400
 and this led to those charges of perjury, you know, that lying under oath.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.280
 But when Representative Waters says what she says,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.160
 she's implying that the Republicans are trying to impeach Clinton

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.240
 for a consensual affair rather than for the perjury itself.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.720
 And this framing, whether it's accurate or not,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.440
 shifted the focus away from the legal charge of perjury

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.320
 and toward a debate about the appropriateness of the relationship.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.960
 Distraction achieved.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.120
 As a little side note, this also kind of showed up

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.680
 when President Trump was being tried in New York City

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.320
 and Stormy Daniels was on the stand.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.000
 You heard some people commenting saying that people just wanted the,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.960
 you know, salacious details of the encounter

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.360
 between Stormy Daniels and the president.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.120
 There was a second distraction tactic that was related to Clinton.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.720
 Here's Senator Dianne Feinstein.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.440
 Well, I've read most of the press from other countries.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.360
 And as you know, it's universal that no one can understand what is happening

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.200
 and that most of the leaders of these other countries

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.880
 really believe that we're tearing at the fabric of the presidency

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.600
 at a time when the United States is really important in the world

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.560
 in terms of peace and stability.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.040
 The world is a more uncertain place now than it has been.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.320
 The world is a more uncertain place now than it has been in the last ten years.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:47.240
 And having a strong American president out there is important.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.640
 And all of this goes to diminish that.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.800
 So I'm one that believes it should be brought to conclusion as quickly as possible.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.600
 And then let's get on with it.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.360
 Did you hear the distraction here?

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.320
 The rest of the world will think less of the United States.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.440
 So if you're debating this, what are you not debating?

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.120
 Whether or not the president committed perjury.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.840
 So what's the takeaway?

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.800
 Well, we live in a world of carefully curated narratives

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.520
 where bad news is eclipsed by shiny distractions

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.560
 and it's amplified by the actual technology we use to consume the information.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.480
 So whether it's a press conference or a news feed

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:28.400
 or even just a casual conversation, remember,

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.160
 you can always look below the surface.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.640
 And one thing that helps journalists look below the surface

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:38.760
 is to get the data behind the story so that they can evaluate it themselves.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.120
 I need to take a quick break, but when I come back,

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.920
 Ben Welsh from Reuters and I talk about the world of data journalism.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.720
 I'll be right back.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.440
 While we're on a break, I wanted to remind you that I have a lot of material

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.480
 like what we have in warm up in my book, Detecting Deception,

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.840
 Tools to Fight Fake News.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:59.680
 I break down more than 20 ways that newsmakers use facts and language

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.200
 to be deceptive, and for each, I give you real world examples to explain

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.720
 and then some historical examples that let you put yourself

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.920
 in the role of a journalist trying to report on these newsmakers.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.000
 There's even answers in the back.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.440
 It's an inexpensive, helpful little book that you might enjoy

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.680
 but in between unspun episodes.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:22.240
 You can find out more at bit.ly/detectingdeceptionbook, all one word.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:28.280
 That's bit.ly/detectingdeceptionbook, all one word.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:29.960
 And now just a quick word from my sponsors

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:34.320
 and then my uninterrupted interview with Ben Welsh.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:35.800
 Welcome back.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.800
 All right, so I'm very excited to have you with us this week on the podcast.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.320
 And one of the things I first wanted to ask you is it seems like your job

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.840
 is really different from the job you might like see a journalist on TV doing,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.680
 right, you know, like in a movie or something.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:53.480
 So it's different from sitting in a press briefing

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:56.680
 and asking questions or talking to someone on the phone.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:00.480
 So first of all, can you just explain what data reporting is?

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.320
 What is data reporting? Great question.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.440
 I think it actually is actually more than one answer.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.120
 And the way to summarize it, like a lot of things in life, is it depends.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.720
 It kind of depends what you do with it.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:14.560
 I mean, the most famous example of a data journalist people might encounter

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.200
 on cable news would be John King or Steve Kranacki

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.600
 or these folks that stand in front of the magic maps on election night

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.520
 and help to decode what the data is telling us about how voters,

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.640
 you know, how voters are behaving.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.840
 But data journalism is a lot more than that, too.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.720
 It's it really is, you know, reporters and editors

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:39.000
 who treat structured information, spreadsheets, maps,

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.920
 big piles of documents as sources in the same way

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:46.040
 that we've traditionally all treated people.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.400
 We can talk to it on the telephone or shoot questions out of the press

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.200
 conference as sources, you know, structured information.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.080
 Data is is a source that in the same ways.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.560
 And we're we're using as data journalists

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:03.080
 just kind of different tools to ask questions and try to get answers

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:06.600
 out of, you know, the data that surrounds us all every day.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.360
 Yeah. So that sounds like it would have a kind of different set of challenges

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.280
 from doing live interviews.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.560
 Would you be willing to give maybe an example of a story

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.000
 that you've worked on that you're particularly proud of? Hmm.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.520
 Well, it's always interesting which one pops into your head.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:27.400
 Well, as a few years ago, I was working as a reporter at the Los Angeles Times

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.400
 and a thing happened in the news.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.360
 A candidate for mayor made an allegation.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:36.040
 He said that, hey, the fire department here in our city is in big trouble

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:38.600
 and you better elect me to fix it.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.040
 Now, obviously, that's a bit of a caricature on my part,

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.080
 but he said something along those lines.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.560
 He actually said it in more vivid terms, made some pretty strong claims

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.120
 about what was happening in our local 911 system.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.000
 And that kind of raised a question in our public sphere in Los Angeles about,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.280
 hey, is the fire department doing a bad job?

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.400
 What's going on over there? Right.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.480
 And that's a question that, you know, with traditional techniques,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.880
 you can answer by just interviewing people,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.800
 you know, going to a fire station and riding along and seeing what's going on.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.720
 And all that is supremely valuable.

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:14.920
 But it's also a question that there's a measurable answer to.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:17.920
 In some cases, you know, it's claims were and as a data journalist,

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.920
 that's often what we're looking to do.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.480
 We have we have questions for the world.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:25.520
 But can we can we measure the answer to help us come up with something

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:29.160
 that's a little maybe more stronger or broader or more contextualized

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:30.840
 than what you could do without it?

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.800
 And so in that case, the claim really was that the response times,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.720
 how long it was taking for the fire department to arrive when you call 911

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.280
 was getting slower was the claim of the candidate.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:42.960
 And hey, that's measurable.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.440
 And so I set out to file a Freedom of Information Act request

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:51.760
 seeking not public documents, not emails or memos, but a database.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.040
 There's a database at the core of the 911 system.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:58.200
 I learned that by interviewing people as a traditional reporter would.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.360
 I filed the request is a longer story, but, you know, I eventually received

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:07.560
 a CD in the mail that had the government database of every 911 call

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:09.240
 for a long period of time.

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:13.120
 And then I set out to to write the computer code necessary

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:17.240
 to sort of get my hands on and interview that database

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.640
 to ask you questions like, hey, how long does it take, say, on average

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:22.400
 for a 911 response to arrive?

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.200
 Hey, is it faster in one part of town versus another?

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.040
 Hey, is it getting slower?

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.560
 Is it getting faster over time? Right.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:34.480
 And and I also sought out outside standards for what might be good or bad.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:39.600
 Hey, the fire department has said their goal is to respond in X amount of time.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.480
 Are they doing that? Are they not, et cetera, et cetera?

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.680
 And so those are all kind of questions that were measurable.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.640
 And then you write the code that kind of spits out the answer,

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:52.720
 you know, not unlike how a social scientist or someone who works

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:58.200
 at a university might for an academic study, just maybe in a simpler way.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:02.920
 And then the answers that we got back from those provided the foundation

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:07.200
 for a whole series of stories we did about problems in the 911 system,

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:10.440
 which led to some pretty significant reforms and how it was managed,

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:15.280
 including changes to how calls were handled, the replacement of the fire chief.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.480
 Ultimately, and a number of changes.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:20.840
 And one fun fact is all the things that we discover and wrote about

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.040
 were not what the candidate was complaining about.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.200
 His complaints were actually inaccurate.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.480
 But by getting the data and figuring it out ourselves with, you know,

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:34.320
 with our technical skills and a more scientific approach,

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.440
 we actually got a lot closer to the truth. Right.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:38.240
 Fair enough.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:42.040
 So one thing that makes me think of is that I know that in journalism,

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:45.400
 a lot of reporters have sort of standard coverage areas that they work in.

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.360
 Right. So that they get to know the sources and they get expertise in that area.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:52.840
 You know, we might call that like beat reporting as a data reporter.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:55.440
 Do you have particular areas of specialization

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.680
 or is it more that you kind of go out and work with other people?

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:00.600
 Some do, some don't.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.520
 You know, I think it really, again, come back to my unsatisfying answer.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:05.560
 It depends.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:09.480
 And so there are data journalists who have areas of really strong expertise.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:13.160
 I think elections is an example of that and political science and polling.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.520
 There's a lot of great data journalists who that's pretty much all they do.

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:19.520
 And they do it very, very well. Right.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:22.080
 And then there's other data journalists who are maybe a little more

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.960
 technical specialists or sort of, you know,

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.760
 social scientists for hire in the newsroom, you know,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.760
 who will range and partner with reporters who have expertise

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:34.120
 in this topic or that topic. Right.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.320
 And juggle a lot of different things over time.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:40.680
 I think in the career of a data journalist, you can end up in either of those situations.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:44.800
 And it often depends on the personality of the person.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.760
 But I think either model can work really very well.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.640
 You do end up developing as this is true of all reporters,

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:56.000
 is you do end up kind of developing expertise in the areas you cover over time.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:59.840
 And that isn't always planned out. It just kind of happens.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:04.120
 OK, it sounds like this kind of projects take a lot of time, too.

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:05.680
 Would that be fair to say?

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:07.360
 They can. Again, it depends.

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:11.320
 You know, I think traditionally data analysis and journalism was developed

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:14.400
 a lot earlier than I think people realize there.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.600
 I mean, there were data stories that were quite scientific in the 19th century,

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:22.120
 including one you can read about, about how a young congressman named

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:25.920
 Abraham Lincoln was submitting the largest expense reports in Congress

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.240
 at the time for travel.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:29.880
 Those were, of course, all done with, you know,

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:32.880
 very traditional analog computational techniques,

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:38.520
 probably pen and paper. And but, you know, even computerized data analysis

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.480
 is older than people realize it in the 1960s and 70s.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:45.480
 You already had a small group of data journalists doing very ambitious

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:49.640
 survey stories and other other works of analysis

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.520
 using punch cards and early computers.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:55.280
 And those projects created kind of an early investigative tradition

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:58.760
 in data journalism, where a lot of the specialists were really focused

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.720
 on long term investigative pieces that took quite a bit of time to get done. Right.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:07.000
 And so that was kind of the origin of a lot of the computational approaches

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:12.280
 to data in news. However, over time, as these tools have gotten easier to use,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.720
 as data has become much more commonplace in our society,

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.120
 you see a lot of data work happening very, very quickly.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:23.240
 And so you can see data stories that happen on a one or two day turnaround.

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:26.360
 You know, when there's an unexpected event where data can help cover it.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:29.960
 And then there's also cases now where, you know, data has become a flowing

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:35.800
 river of news where every day when a new macro economic number comes across

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:39.480
 the wire from the Bureau of Labor Statistics that can feed into

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:44.080
 not just traditional stories that a data journalist would report and put out,

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:49.560
 but also maybe into software applications you're making to automatically publish new stories.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.520
 So you're saying you actually do write software as a part of your job?

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.960
 I do not all data journalists do, but I definitely do.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:00.720
 An example that I think almost all of your listeners will be familiar with.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.600
 There's two. One is election night.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.840
 So, you know, the most popular piece of journalism on election night now

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:12.640
 and pretty much since about 2008 have been the live updating election maps

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:14.320
 that give you the results as they come in.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:19.720
 Almost all of those systems were invented and developed by people who started

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.560
 their careers as news reporters and editors and kind of drifted into software

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:27.840
 development or sought it out because it does take quite a bit of editorial

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.360
 kind of knowledge to be able to make something like that correctly.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.240
 Another example that's more recent is a coronavirus tracking.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:40.280
 So I'm sure almost everyone who's listening looked at, you know, a daily

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:43.720
 updating map or chart, at least with some frequency of the latest

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.640
 information on coronavirus where they live.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:51.960
 Those were also cases where data journalists were kind of filling the void

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:55.560
 of where the government information wasn't satisfactory and developing

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.160
 software to kind of put news out automatically.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:02.000
 Yeah. So you bring up, I think, an important thing here, which is

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:06.240
 it sounds like this kind of reporting is pretty resource intensive

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:09.000
 in terms of sort of the human resources, the time, the expertise.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.320
 People have to build up those kind of things.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:17.000
 So I guess what I'm getting at is what makes it worth it.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.720
 So you've brought up kind of one thing, which is that you can kind of fill in

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:23.320
 gaps, right, telling stories that are not going to come out otherwise.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.720
 Are there other things that kind of make the investment worth it?

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.800
 You're right. It is higher skill work.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:31.400
 And we've seen this in both data and graphics journalism over the last two

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:34.760
 decades is that they've gone from jobs that were often about creating

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:38.800
 small accessories to go with stories to making these much more sophisticated

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:44.480
 feature stories, large scale investigations and these data applications.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:46.280
 So it's definitely higher skill work.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:49.680
 You hit on one of the major payoffs is like, yeah, it lets you do the big

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.560
 story you couldn't have got otherwise.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:55.000
 Right. And that's been the reason this field really started to develop

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:56.920
 in the first place decades ago.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:59.800
 But you have to remember is that once you automate something

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.960
 and put it on the Internet, you can have quite a bit of scale. Right.

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:06.560
 And so, for instance,

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:09.640
 when I was an editor at the Los Angeles Times, we created a system

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:13.240
 to automatically draft blog posts every time there was an earthquake.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:17.120
 The government data would come across and we would have within seconds

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:20.760
 a post with a map that had the bare facts about the earthquake

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:24.360
 that was reviewed by a human editor, of course, but was ready to go out

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:27.720
 before everyone else for this, an event that would draw a lot of attention.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.440
 Well, yeah, it did take us a few months to invent that application,

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.440
 finish it, vet it and prepare it.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:38.080
 But once it was running, it was close to zero labor whatsoever. Right.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:41.800
 And it then every time there was an earthquake

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:46.520
 yielded a piece of content that was oftentimes of very high value. Right.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:50.600
 And so you have a large investment at the outset. Right.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:56.160
 But once it's operational, it can have quite a bit of long running value

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:59.160
 and a really wide amount of scale.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:04.600
 The third thing is is a lot of these data features are actually significantly

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:09.080
 sometimes by an order of magnitude or more

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:13.800
 more popular than a traditional text and photo story. Right.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.360
 The coronavirus trackers, for instance, that were published by news

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:22.200
 organizations were in almost every case, the most popular piece of content

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:27.760
 ever published in the history of the news organizations that published them.

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:31.560
 That's these are like at the Los Angeles Times, our coronavirus tracker

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:38.360
 was the most read thing ever published by the L.A. Times in 130 years. Right.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:42.720
 Of thousands of people spending how many work hours creating product.

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:44.840
 And so, yes, it is higher skill work.

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:48.200
 And yes, it does require more time than writing an individual story.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:53.240
 But it also can be significantly more valuable.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:58.440
 OK, so that makes a good business case, I guess, for my investment.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:02.560
 This is why this is me to my boss saying, hey, let me hire some people.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:04.480
 OK, that's fair enough.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:07.840
 It does bring up another question, though, because I think about, you know,

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:12.400
 a larger resource publication or TV station like a CNN or L.A.

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:16.480
 Times versus maybe like your local news or your local newspaper.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:19.640
 Do you think that the ability to produce these kinds of stories

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:22.760
 sort of creates an information divide between national and local?

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.520
 It definitely does, yes, because it requires this higher amount of skill

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:29.640
 to publish it. It can be hard for a small newsroom to put it together.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:33.280
 So the attempts to solve that in the marketplace have been, of course,

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:37.040
 like everything else with local news, chain ownership, you know what I mean?

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:41.200
 And so the Gannett Company and these sort of large chains that own dozens

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:45.360
 and dozens, if not hundreds of local newsrooms have experimented

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:50.040
 with having sort of centralized teams that, you know, provide this type

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:51.800
 of work for the entire chain.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.040
 You see that also in the television marketplace.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:58.320
 So CBS News here in New York has a growing data team they're hiring

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:02.080
 for right now, and they'll perform data analysis and work for all

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:05.240
 of the owned and operated CBS stations across the country.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:08.520
 So centralization is definitely one solution.

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:12.840
 Obviously, I should pump here a little bit for the wire services.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:16.040
 I work at Reuters News, which is one of the world's largest agency

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:20.560
 and wire services, and so subscribers, you know, to our wire service,

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:25.400
 any outlet are able to access a lot of these graphics that we make automatically

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:27.520
 using some of these things I've talked about.

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:31.000
 And our live election results will publish this November

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:35.120
 with the national election pool are available to subscribers to our wire.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:38.680
 So if you're if you're a member of Reuters News, you'll be able to take

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:42.360
 at a bare minimum the sort of finished maps and tables that we create

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:44.760
 and put them on your site or on your TV station.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:46.480
 So those are two ways it works.

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:49.960
 There are some nonprofit academic alternatives to this.

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:53.480
 I spent a year at Stanford University recently working

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:58.480
 on a project called Big Local News, and the intention of this project is

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:04.120
 to create sort of a a similar model, a similar

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:08.120
 centralized kind of model for providing data services and skills

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:11.200
 and support to newsrooms in the nonprofit sector.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:14.440
 So a lot of, you know, a lot of the small newsrooms that are coming up

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:18.160
 on the nonprofit side who don't have access to the things

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:20.160
 these these larger commercial chains do.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:24.600
 OK, so I want to switch gears a little bit here

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:29.680
 and ask you about the particular data sets and things that you access.

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:32.280
 And can you talk a little bit about how you try to verify

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:35.640
 sort of the reliability of the data that you get access to?

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.280
 Sure. I mean, it can depend because there's there's different types of data sets,

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:43.320
 you know, and there's different ways they're kind of created.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:48.040
 But to use the 911 data set as an example, we started with.

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:51.320
 So I have via a Freedom of Information Act quest,

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:55.600
 the official database from the primary source. Right.

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:58.480
 So in a way, that's the same as interviewing the buyer chief.

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:02.960
 There's no there's no intermediary who might introduce error. Right.

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:05.880
 Now, there are cases where you might get a data set from an intermediary

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:09.800
 and that could introduce some question about the kind of chain of custody.

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:12.920
 But in this case, it was what I would call a primary source.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:17.560
 But it's, you know, 15 database tables that are largely undocumented.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:21.600
 And not a lot of people at the agency are all that willing to explain to me

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:25.200
 how they fit together and how they work and what the caveats are of.

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:28.880
 Oh, gee, you need to filter and exclude all these and oh, gee,

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.880
 be careful when you merge these two things and all the crazy stuff

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:34.360
 that can happen with databases. Right.

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:36.640
 And what do these fields and columns even mean?

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:38.120
 They have these cryptic names.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:41.760
 And so there often is in a circumstance like that in data journalism,

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:45.440
 a bit of forensic analysis you do to just sort of try to gradually

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:48.400
 test and deduce what the heck is in there.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:52.000
 That can include interviewing people who have worked on the system,

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:53.160
 which I did in that case.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.840
 We found an ex employee who had worked on the database

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.600
 and someone who had done data entry on the database.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:01.400
 And we kind of were able to decode what was in there

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.400
 and understand better how to interpret it.

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:09.200
 Oftentimes, just finding the form that's used to create a database

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.240
 can really help you understand what's in there

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:14.960
 because, you know, that has a little more of a human sort of base to it.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:21.000
 And then to me, there's sort of, I guess, lacking a better term,

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:27.320
 I would say kind of external validation or checking, checking, checking my work.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:30.480
 And so in the case of the fire system, I had some questions

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:33.560
 I wanted to answer that were really specifically about our story

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:37.000
 and like, hey, are the response times meeting this national standard?

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:39.800
 Are are things getting faster, things getting slow?

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:41.720
 That's what I wanted to know at the end of the day.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:45.520
 But those those didn't help me verify whether I was right or not. Right.

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.120
 And so what I did is I sought out

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:52.040
 data analysis that had been published by the department itself

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:56.440
 in its annual reports and presented at hearings before the city council.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:59.640
 So in the past, the fire department had added up.

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:02.560
 We did this many total calls over the whole year.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:06.400
 And these sort of general aggregate statistics have been published.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:12.680
 And I sort of hypothesized or thought, hey, if I'm analyzing this database correctly,

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.400
 right, I should be able to hit those numbers, right?

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:21.160
 Using whatever sort of queries or formulas I'm going to use to analyze the data set.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:25.280
 And that gave me some confidence that, hey, I'm able to interpret this data

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:29.800
 without making a lot of it, you know, without making a mistake in how I'm working with it.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.880
 And then, you know, in a case like this, an investigative report,

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.280
 all of our findings were presented

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:40.880
 to the agency prior to being published.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.840
 And so I'm a big fan of when publishing

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:47.760
 sort of claims that are that are going to that are going to get out on a limb

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:51.320
 a little bit or challenge a source or subject,

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:55.600
 is that they have the opportunity to review, comment, critique on the method

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:59.160
 and the numbers prior to publication, which I did in that.

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:01.480
 There's other stories I've been down that road on it to me.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:03.440
 That's a really important step.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:05.640
 And we did that.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:11.480
 And but, you know, even at the end of the day, there sometimes can be a little

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:16.600
 you might not get a hundred percent answer

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:18.720
 from your source about whether or not you've done it right,

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:23.240
 because you may be breaking new ground in terms of, you know, your findings.

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:27.080
 And so a really important piece

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:30.640
 that I think is often overlooked in a lot of the data journalism today

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:34.360
 that's done very quickly or by people who don't have a journalism background

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:39.520
 is shoe leather ground truthing the data, which is basically saying, OK,

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:44.200
 so we did this analysis that was like these this this part of the call system

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:46.400
 is really slow and it's causing problems.

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:50.200
 Well, if that's right, I should be able to sort and list the calls

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:53.600
 that are the worst, the outliers that were that are going to be inside

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:55.440
 this kind of claim that we're making.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:59.000
 And we should be able to call up the people on the phone or knock on the door

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:03.280
 of the people who are listed there and be able to verify that.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:04.840
 Yeah, it was slow. Right.

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:08.480
 And so to me, it was taking the data out into the real world. Right.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:12.640
 To verify that, hey, our assumption is that this is pointing to a thing

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:14.880
 that happened in the world. Did it? Right.

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:19.080
 And that requires getting, you know, unplugging your laptop, closing the lid,

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.640
 you know, getting out onto the street, knocking on doors, talking to people

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:27.200
 and really checking whether things you match up out there in the real world.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:31.440
 And I think that that is an underrated and essential piece

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.560
 of doing quality data journalism.

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:37.440
 It also helps you find the stories and real people who are affected

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:41.200
 by the statistical stuff that you're writing about.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:44.400
 And you end up with a much, much better piece of journalism.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:48.800
 And you also can have an even greater degree of confidence

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:54.280
 that what you're writing about matters, whether or not it, quote, adds up.

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:57.200
 Yeah. So just to go back to a term that you used,

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.280
 you use the term chain of custody.

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:01.680
 You got it directly from the horse's mouth. Right.

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:05.760
 So there's cases where this happens frequently with document dumps

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:10.320
 and other types of data stories where you get the information

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.920
 from an intermediary, a leaker, right, or there's been a hack.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.920
 And you may remember this from the coverage of the WikiLeaks documents

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:20.520
 and things about a decade ago.

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:24.000
 Questions were raised about whether the information had been tampered with.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.360
 Right. And can we really believe that this email that says is from Hillary Clinton

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:31.840
 is really from Hillary Clinton? Right.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.480
 And those are cases where you have to make extra steps

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:37.720
 to verify that what you have is is is authentic.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:40.400
 But in the case where you file a Freedom of Information Act

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:43.760
 directly to a government agency and they give it to you directly,

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:45.760
 you don't have that intermediary.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:48.480
 The human equivalent of this, and this is true in data journalism,

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:52.960
 almost anything we would talk about in this wonky, techie data way.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:56.200
 There almost always is a shoe leather on the street,

00:31:56.400 --> 00:32:01.000
 old school reporter version of is, hey, you went to the protest

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:05.120
 where things really got crazy and did you see the crazy thing that happened?

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:08.520
 Did you talk to the person who the crazy thing happened to

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:11.360
 or did you talk to the person who heard from the person

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.160
 the crazy thing happened to? Right.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:14.320
 And it's the same thing.

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:16.760
 And this is true in all different aspects of journalism,

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:20.200
 is how close are you really to the to the real thing?

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:25.160
 Right. To what actually happened and this and in data that's no different.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:29.520
 How do you balance all of those technical wonky things you just talked about

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.680
 with the need to tell a really interesting story

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:34.880
 maybe to a public who's often number phobic?

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.520
 I mean, connecting it to real people and real things that happened,

00:32:38.520 --> 00:32:40.480
 I think is is essential.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.880
 I think it is missing from a lot of data reporting.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:47.440
 However, I think there's more appetite for data than I think is appreciated.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:51.880
 You know, I think that properly balanced reported and presented

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.880
 data stories are really very popular.

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:59.520
 And I think that not to not to hit this nail too many times in our talk here today,

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:02.280
 but I think that that is underappreciated.

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:05.920
 I think there's oftentimes a sense that these are vegetable stories

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:09.560
 when in fact, when we look at the look at the numbers and the metrics,

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:14.240
 we see that these are actually quite popular stories when done well.

00:33:14.240 --> 00:33:18.200
 Right. The public wants information that's more trustworthy and reliable

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:22.680
 and data and science is a way that we bring that to our journalism.

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:26.200
 Right. To me, there's a lot of it comes down to the editing.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:28.840
 And this is true in traditional stories as well.

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:33.200
 Is data stories can sometimes lack that ground truth thing

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:35.760
 and that connection to what's really out there. Right.

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:40.160
 They can sometimes be what in a in a traditional story

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:42.120
 would be called a notebook dump.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.640
 You know, so, hey, I did a data analysis.

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:47.600
 Here's 15 things I found from looking at the data.

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:49.480
 And it's just like a bunch of stuff.

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:53.720
 And it's not clearly focused enough that it connects directly

00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:57.880
 with a busy person who who is just trying to go through the headlines and read the news.

00:33:58.080 --> 00:34:01.960
 And so to me, the best data stories, not always,

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:06.960
 but oftentimes are about one finding or one very simple statistic.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:10.840
 And then they don't overload all the numbers and stuff in the way

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:14.960
 that there's there's a good reason that academic papers do that. Right.

00:34:15.160 --> 00:34:20.600
 And I do think a well presented data story will offer, you know, below the fold,

00:34:20.600 --> 00:34:24.480
 so to speak, in the news or on a link that goes off to a methodology page

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:26.680
 or some code and data that you posted online.

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:30.280
 It will provide that extra stuff for the reader who wants more of it.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:34.840
 And to verify it's kind of it's it's reproducibility.

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:39.360
 But the headline, what we call news, the nut graph,

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:43.920
 the central finding of any piece needs to be almost always like one thing

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:47.120
 with maybe just one or two numbers that really connect

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:51.440
 directly as possible to the reader. Why is this news, you know?

00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:55.280
 Yeah. And so I guess that would explain then why there's often

00:34:55.280 --> 00:34:58.040
 a big series around a large data story, right?

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:00.240
 Each one is telling a different piece of it.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.000
 It can be, yes. Yeah. All right.

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:06.800
 What are some of the biggest challenges that you face in doing this kind of work?

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:11.320
 Gee, I mean, it does require more technical skills

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:14.240
 than I think a lot of people receive in their journalism training.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:18.440
 You know, over time, it's something that any person could learn.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:23.760
 Being a data journalist is not like being an opera singer or the piano player

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:28.120
 at the orchestra. It's not something that requires you're born

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:29.880
 with some incredible innate talent.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:32.800
 It just requires that you learn

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:37.440
 numeracy and kind of set of kind of spreadsheet like skills

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:42.280
 for how to get your hooks into clean up, deal with and interview a data source.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:46.040
 And so I think that that is its own little educational ladder

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:49.280
 that you have to climb to to do.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.280
 But I think it's really attainable to anyone.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.040
 But I think starting out, that can be a challenge, you know,

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:57.600
 in the same way that starting out at any professional job

00:35:57.600 --> 00:36:00.320
 requires a little bit of of learning.

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:05.240
 The data that is often most newsworthy is often difficult to acquire.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:09.600
 So it's there's cases where, you know, the government has a data set

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:12.200
 that you want to do a story about, but they're not giving it to you.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:14.160
 And so you got to figure out how to get it.

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:18.960
 And so that kind of struggle to get the data that will make the news

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:23.920
 is a challenge. Cleaning data, as anyone who works in what is now

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:28.200
 known as data science and a lot of kind of these more practical applications

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:31.880
 of statistics will tell you, cleaning the data can end up being

00:36:31.880 --> 00:36:33.960
 about 95 percent of the work.

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:36.960
 So the actual analysis that you do can be quite simple,

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:38.840
 especially for a journalism story.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:42.560
 But once you even have acquired the data, just getting it into shape

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:45.200
 where it's ready to analyze can be a ton of work

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:49.120
 just due to errors in how it's been structured or formatted.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:54.600
 That the need to to kind of clean up values, all the different ways

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:57.480
 that you can spell the street names of New York or L.A.

00:36:57.760 --> 00:37:01.200
 If you're a police officer filling out a citation form, right,

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:04.200
 can lead to quite a lot of variations that need to be mopped up.

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:07.960
 So cleaning is in and learning the skills of cleaning

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:11.680
 and how to do it correctly and all that can be a bit of a challenge.

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:14.480
 And it's oftentimes most of the job.

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.800
 All right. And I appreciate your time and thank you so much for stopping by.

